WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:06.120
The best animated features go that extra mile
to immerse viewers, helping the audience forget

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.100
that the characters they're investing in have
been drawn in pencil or rendered with pixels.

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:16.800
All of that hard work is so easily undone
by a simple mistake, however, and they happen

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.900
at the top level way more often than you'd
think.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:23.780
Are a few misplaced pixels going to ruin a
great animated movie?

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:24.280
Not at all.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:28.860
Mistakes are unavoidable, but when you do
actually notice one of these errors, it ends

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.320
up becoming impossible to unsee.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.300
Toy Story

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.500
Pixar's surprise hit Toy Story blew audiences
away in 1995.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:43.820
It outperformed blockbusters like GoldenEye,
Batman Forever, and Jumanji at the box office

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:49.050
to become the highest-grossing film of the
year, and taught us all some valuable lessons

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:50.320
about playing with toys.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:59.840
"We toys can see everything, so play nice."

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.940
But this critically acclaimed smash hit isn't
completely perfect.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:09.320
The mistakes begin during the opening credits
as Andy plays with his toys - he drives RV

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.510
into his makeshift cardboard bank, but in
the next scene he comes out of the closet.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.800
The mistakes continue as Buzz Lightyear arrives
on the scene.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.550
As the clueless Space Ranger attempts to take
flight, he slides down a Hot Wheels track

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:26.040
and does a full loop, though this clearly
wouldn't have been possible — the shot from

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.640
his point of view reveals that his wingspan
is way too wide.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.760
"That wasn't flying, that was falling with
style."

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:35.340
The Lion King

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:42.240
Just one year before Toy Story changed animation
forever, Disney released The Lion King.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:47.920
This truly memorable movie smashed previous
records to become the highest grossing hand-drawn

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.730
film of all time and went on to become the
best-selling VHS ever.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:57.390
According to The Daily Beast, the House of
Mouse didn't even have its best animators

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:01.140
on the job, with the A-team all assigned to
Pocahontas instead.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:05.180
Maybe that's why the animation often has some
jarring mistakes.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.860
For example, the "whites" of Simba's eye turn
yellow in certain scenes.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.080
Nala's eyes switch from blue to green at different
points in the movie, too.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:16.680
Shrek

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.740
DreamWorks' big breakthrough Shrek wasn't
afraid to lean heavily into the realm of toilet

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:27.299
humor, and that was one of the things that
made it such a huge hit for the studio.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.840
Shrek subverts the typical Disney tropes right
from the get-go.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:37.140
"Wake up!"

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:38.000
"What?"

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.780
Even the opening credits are quick to establish
that this isn't your typical hero.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:47.620
Unfortunately, the animators must have been
having so much fun showing Shrek brush his

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.000
teeth with insect guts that they forgot right
from left.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.200
In the scene, Shrek creates a "Beware Ogre"
sign for his swamp, but did you ever notice

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.020
the mistake here?

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.639
When he's painting the sign, we can see from
behind it that the top left corner is missing,

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:05.519
but when he plants it in the ground and we
see it from the front, it's the top right

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.380
corner that's missing.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:07.900
Does it ruin the movie?

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.480
Hardly, but good luck not noticing it whenever
the scene comes up.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.120
"I guess you don't entertain much, do you?"

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.840
"I like my privacy."

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:16.780
"you know I do too!"

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.540
"That's another thing we have in common."

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.060
Monsters, Inc.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:26.500
The tiny details that the animators of 2001's
Monsters, Inc. had to contend with took up

00:03:26.519 --> 00:03:31.490
a lot of their time, which is probably why
some glaringly obvious mistakes managed to

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.120
make it into the final cut.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:38.380
During one scene, hapless scarer George Sanderson
emerges from a child's bedroom with a little

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:41.840
white sock stuck to his shoulder, triggering
a red alert.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.040
"George and I are like brothers!"

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.180
"CODE 2319!"

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.320
WE HAVE A 2319!

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:53.620
The Child Detection Agency show up to dispose
of the supposedly dangerous item with a controlled

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.360
explosion, shaving the monster bald for good
measure.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:11.260
It's a fun, but ultimately flawed scene, because
when Sanderson is pinned down by the CDA,

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.040
the toxic sock has somehow flipped over.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.440
The Lego Batman Movie

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:22.620
It might have fallen well short of 2014's
The Lego Movie in terms of box office returns,

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.580
but The Lego Batman Movie still raked in a
ton of cash globally.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:29.900
Critics and audiences loved it, even if it
isn't a perfect movie.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.880
Some of the mistakes made by the animators
get lost in the madness of the movie's many

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:40.350
action set pieces, but when The Lego Batman
Movie slows down, you do start to notice the

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:40.900
cracks a little.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.820
During the scene in which Bruce Wayne talks
to the portrait of his dead parents, the pictures

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:48.220
to either side change around between shots.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:49.000
"Look at that!

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:50.600
The old gang."

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:52.280
Frozen

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:56.900
Disney's Frozen was always likely to do big
business.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:01.620
The studio must have known it was on to a
winner just based on the incredibly catchy

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:06.520
songs, but nobody could have predicted that
Frozen would take over the world the way it

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:07.020
did.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.960
Elsa is the star of the show, but her sister
Anna also seems to be capable of a little

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:12.460
magic.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.640
Apparently, she can let solid objects pass
right through her body.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.940
After she realizes that Kristoff has been
the one for her all along, Anna surprises

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.720
him with a brand new sled to replace the one
he lost while helping her.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:30.560
In his euphoria, Kristoff picks Anna up by
the waist and gives her a romantic little

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.270
spin, but hit your pause button mid-swirl
and you'll see that his thumb actually goes

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:36.600
through Anna's waist.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:40.880
It's a blink-and-you-miss-it moment, but now
that you know about it you won't be able to

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:41.380
unsee it.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:43.240
Wreck-It Ralph

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:48.780
This colorful Disney hit wowed the majority
of critics when it came out in 2012.

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:53.960
However, just like the '80s arcade games it
features, Wreck-It Ralph comes with the odd

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.890
glitch, and we're not talking about Vanellope's
predicament.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:01.300
"But there's one who cannot escape because
she's a glitch!"

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:04.340
"Help, somebody help!"

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.960
In the opening moments of the movie, Ralph
explains his job within the game Fix-It Felix,

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:14.570
Jr — essentially, he wrecks the game's single
building, while the hero, Felix, is tasked

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:15.060
with fixing it.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.620
"So yeah naturally the guy with the name 'Fix-It
Felix'."

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.780
He's nice enough as good guys go

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:22.920
"He definitely fixes stuff really well."

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.180
We get a quick run-through of how the game
works, and at the end of the explanation,

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.680
Felix is awarded with a medal while Ralph
gets tossed off the top of the building by

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:32.880
the Nicelanders.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.780
If you look closely, there's something wrong
with this scene.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:41.250
From a wide angle, we see that Ralph is standing
right next to the ledge during the ceremony,

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:46.060
yet in the close-up shot that follows he's
now square in the middle, giving the residents

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:48.720
of the apartment building enough room for
a run-up.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.770
Someone's mama probably wasn't too proud of
that moment.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:58.520
"You wanna go pee pee in your big boy slacks,
it's make your mama proud time!"

00:06:58.520 --> 00:06:59.200
"I love my mama!"

